findings.recommendation:
Add to both nginx server blocks (client/nginx.conf and the deploy.yml heredoc, keeping them identical):
```nginx
add_header X-Frame-Options "DENY" always;
add_header Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'" always;
add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;
add_header Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin" always;
```
frane-ancestors 'none' is the part that closes the clickjacking path independently of SameSite. Verify the CSP against the built bundle before shipping (Tailwind v4 + Vite may need 'unsafe-inline' for styles; if so, keep script-src strict and relax style-src only). Confirm separately that the fronting proxy emits Strict-Transport-Security, or add it here.

Add a limit_req zone at the nginx layer for the auth endpoints, which is the cheapest place and requires no Go changes:
```nginx
limit_req_zone $binary_remote_addr zone=auth:10m rate=10r/m;
location = /auth/yandex/code { limit_req zone=auth burst=5 nodelay; ... }
location = /auth/refresh     { limit_req zone=auth burst=5 nodelay; ... }
```
applied identically in client/nginx.conf and the deploy.yml heredoc. A modest global limit on /api/ is worth adding too. If in-process limiting is preferred later, a small token-bucket middleware keyed by X-Real-IP (already set by both nginx blocks) fits the existing middleware pattern.

Validate at the consumption point in yandexAuth.js:
```js
const isSafeInternalPath = (value) =>
  typeof value === "string" && value.startsWith("/") && !value.startsWith("//");

export const getAuthRedirectTarget = () => {
  const stored = consumeAuthReturnTo();
  if (isSafeInternalPath(stored)) return stored;
  return import.meta.env.VITE_AUTH_SUCCESS_REDIRECT || "/";
};
```
Rejecting a leading "//" (and, defensively, any value containing "\\") closes it. Validating on write in saveAuthReturnTo as well makes the invariant hold on both sides.
